---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { getGuide } from "../../data/guides";

const guide = getGuide("demo");
---

<DocsLayout guide={guide}>
  <section class="docs-hero docs-searchable" id="overview">
    <p class="docs-kicker">Report example</p>
    <h1>Read a scan.<span>Before you run anything.</span></h1>
    <p>
      Open the <a href="./sample/sample.html">offline HTML report</a> to see how Repyy presents findings, source locations, and coverage.
      It was generated from 11 inert risky fixtures and 11 paired controls with a local build of the 0.6.0 code (<code>0.6.0-dev</code>).
      The fixtures are data; the runner invoked only Repyy. The build is a prerelease preview, not an attested release binary.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-summary">
      <span><b>11 / 11</b>Expected signals found</span><span><b>0 / 11</b>Controls flagged high or critical</span><span><b>0</b>Incomplete scans</span>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-callout">
      <strong>Start with the report.</strong>
      <p>Open the <a href="./sample/sample.html">HTML view</a>, then compare the <a href="./sample/sample.txt">terminal view</a> or <a href="./sample/sample.json">JSON</a>. The <a href="./sample/results.json">case results</a> include every observed rule and control finding.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="reading-the-report">
    <h2>Three things to read first</h2>
    <div class="docs-table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="How to read a Repyy report" tabindex="0">
      <table class="docs-data-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Report field</th><th>What it tells you</th><th>Next step</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Verdict</td><td>Whether the findings require review or the scan is incomplete.</td><td>Use it to triage, then inspect the underlying evidence.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Rule and location</td><td>What matched and where Repyy found it.</td><td>Read the source at that line; open the <a href="/rules/">rule reference</a> for legitimate-use guidance.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Coverage</td><td>Files scanned, skipped files, and limits reached.</td><td>Treat an incomplete scan as unresolved, even if it has no relevant findings.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="paired-examples">
    <h2>Try three paired examples</h2>
    <p>These fixtures show what a signal looks like beside a nearby benign use. None of the fixture code or dependencies is executed or installed.</p>
    <div class="docs-table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Paired demo fixtures" tabindex="0">
      <table class="docs-data-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Risky fixture</th><th>Expected signal</th><th>Paired control</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>A JSON manifest declares <code>process-log</code>.</td><td><a href="/intelligence/">Sourced package indicator</a></td><td>An ordinary fixture package declaration.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>An unused function passes Axios response data to <code>eval</code>.</td><td><a href="/rules/#rule-FLOW-001">Fetch-to-execution flow</a></td><td>An Axios response returned as data.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Numeric literals encode a credential-path marker.</td><td><a href="/rules/#rule-DECODE-001">Decoded literal evidence</a></td><td>Numeric literals spelling “Hello”.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Eight earlier fixture pairs still cover lifecycle scripts, startup configuration, disguised assets, editor tasks, Git hooks, download strings, credential-path references, and Docker socket mounts.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="reproduce">
    <h2>Reproduce the inert report</h2>
    <p>From a Repyy source checkout, build only Repyy and run its fixture runner. The runner creates temporary text files and removes them after scanning.</p>
    <pre class="docs-example"><code>go build -trimpath -ldflags '-X main.version=0.6.0-dev' -o /tmp/repyy-demo ./cmd/repyy
python3 scripts/benchmark.py --binary /tmp/repyy-demo --output /tmp/repyy-demo-output --check
</code></pre>
    <p>Outputs include <code>sample.html</code>, <code>sample.txt</code>, <code>sample.json</code>, <code>results.json</code>, and <code>results.md</code>. The runner does not invoke npm, Node, Git in a fixture, Docker, an IDE task, or any fixture program.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="corpus-context">
    <h2>What the real corpus adds</h2>
    <p>
      A separate static review of the <a href="https://github.com/xndbogdan/malicious-repositories/tree/dc82f332dae0f4e9ea6bdc1d8341c7743c59913f">pinned malicious-repositories corpus</a>
      examined 15 targets with Repyy 0.5.3-dev. Thirteen scans completed; two were incomplete with four skipped files.
      The review exposed a missing <code>process-log</code> indicator, an Axios-response-to-<code>eval</code> path that was not correlated, and noisy vendor bundles.
      Those observations motivated the focused fixtures above. A separately reviewed, isolated run is still needed before publishing 0.6.0 corpus measurements.
    </p>
    <p><a href="https://github.com/ossf/malicious-packages">OpenSSF Malicious Packages</a> is a source of package advisory reports, separate from the repository corpus. Neither source proves remote payload contents or a common actor.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="limits">
    <h2>Keep the limits in view</h2>
    <p>
      Eleven paired fixtures are a regression check, not a general accuracy score. A clean control means only that it received no high or critical finding in this run;
      lower-severity findings remain in <a href="./sample/results.json">the results</a>. Repyy can miss behavior, flag legitimate code, or leave files uninspected.
      <a href="/coverage/">Read the coverage guide</a> before using a scan to decide whether to trust a repository.
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
